{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-333102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=3\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx=0.25,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **general form** of $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ in terms of the new values ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and the current value $y?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side to define $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2} \\\\ {{-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.25$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f93c637d50220ec61e92dfd153e31ad7a624b5f7f8202995153313e13775960"} {"question" "q-333102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=-\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}+y+0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.25{y}^{2}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-y+0.5\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.25{y}^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-y+0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.25\\,{x}_{\\text{new}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-325128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-x,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-325128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-1+2 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-325128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f93c637d50220ec61e92dfd153e31ad7a624b5f7f8202995153313e13775960"} {"question" "q-325128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-325128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-325128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2} \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-2-(2)^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-2-4 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f93c637d50220ec61e92dfd153e31ad7a624b5f7f8202995153313e13775960"} {"question" "q-333120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-2"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-6"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+6"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333118" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\,{y}^{2}-y,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx=0.5,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333118" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {{1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+0.5=1.5.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5\\cdot 1.5\\,({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1 \\\\  & =1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333118" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f93c637d50220ec61e92dfd153e31ad7a624b5f7f8202995153313e13775960"} {"question" "q-333118" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333118" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}+1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{x}_{\\text{new}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333118" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx=0.5,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of $g$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1+0.5=1.5.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2+0.5(1.5)^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2+1.125 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.875.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f93c637d50220ec61e92dfd153e31ad7a624b5f7f8202995153313e13775960"} {"question" "q-333115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.875"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.875"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+0.875"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.875"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.875"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-x{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(0.5).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(0.5)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0.5,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0.5-0=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+0.5=0.5.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5(0.5)({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-0.5(0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.25(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1.125}{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈0.916667 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈0.915476 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈0.915476\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0.5)≈0.92,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f93c637d50220ec61e92dfd153e31ad7a624b5f7f8202995153313e13775960"} {"question" "q-333263" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333263" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.92"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333250" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-{y}^{2},\\,y(0.5)=0.7\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333250" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5(1){y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.7 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+0.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=0.7$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})+0.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-0.7}{({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}+0.5}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0.5)=0.7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =0.7 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈0.7875 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈0.7845 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈0.7845\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈0.78,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333250" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f93c637d50220ec61e92dfd153e31ad7a624b5f7f8202995153313e13775960"} {"question" "q-333250" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333250" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.78"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(0.4).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(0.4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0.4,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0.4-0=0.4.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(3({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =1.2({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+1.2({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-1.2({x}_{\\text{new}})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.4$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-1.2({x}_{\\text{new}}).\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+0.4=0.4.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-1.2(0.4) \\\\  & =0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.8({y}_{\\text{new}})+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.4(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1.48}{0.8(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.044444 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.044014 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.044014\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0.4)≈1.04,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f93c637d50220ec61e92dfd153e31ad7a624b5f7f8202995153313e13775960"} {"question" "q-333282" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333282" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.04"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-325137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}-{y}^{3},\\,y(0.5)=1\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-325137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-(1)^{2} \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-2}{1.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0.5)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.2 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.179747 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.179509\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈1.18,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-325137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f93c637d50220ec61e92dfd153e31ad7a624b5f7f8202995153313e13775960"} {"question" "q-325137" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-325137" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.18"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-{y}^{2},\\,y(0.4)=1.2\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(0.9).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(0.9)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0.9,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0.9-0.4=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}}).\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.4+0.5=0.9.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.2-(0.9) \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1.2$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-2.1}{({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0.4)=1.2,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1.2 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.281818 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.280351 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.280351\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0.9)≈1.28,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f93c637d50220ec61e92dfd153e31ad7a624b5f7f8202995153313e13775960"} {"question" "q-333286" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "q-333286" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.28"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/state.json" "file_sha256" "f50e2158d16f3e430188beee745393be38c085ecb8209494f1baa57ec0cd1b56"} {"question" "e-21317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = y^3 - x\\,y, \\qquad y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x = 1,$ the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = (y_\\text{new})^3 - 3y_\\text{new} - 1$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = 3y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + 1$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = 3y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 - 1$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/example-21317.json" "file_sha256" "accf7a352b6c6b3aac97e871af52037f83541c9b771bb8ac5acc798befc68fdf"} {"question" "e-21317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $\\Delta x = 1$ is\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new} - y.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}-1 \\\\  & =3{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/example-21317.json" "file_sha256" "accf7a352b6c6b3aac97e871af52037f83541c9b771bb8ac5acc798befc68fdf"} {"question" "e-21319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy + y^3, \\qquad y(1.5)=-0.3\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2).$ Round your answer to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/example-21319.json" "file_sha256" "2e08244774a834d0217cc86557cdf4901724efddadc65d24e8934f4ca1743e0f"} {"question" "e-21319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1.5 = 0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & ={x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5(y_\\text{new})^3 + (x_\\text{new}-1)y_\\text{new} + y = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $\\Delta x = 0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.5(y_\\text{new})^3 + (x_\\text{new}-1)y_\\text{new} + y.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1.5 + 0.5 = 2.$ The old $y$ value is $y = -0.3.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+(2-1){y}_{\\text{new}}-0.3 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=-0.3$ to solve $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{g(y_\\text{new})}{g'(y_\\text{new})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\ng'(y_\\text{new}) = 1.5(y_\\text{new})^2 + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{0.5((y_\\text{new})_n)^3 + (y_\\text{new})_n - 0.3}{1.5((y_\\text{new})_n)^2 + 1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1.5) = -0.3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =-0.3 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈0.240529 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈0.288849 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈0.288050\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx 0.29,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/example-21319.json" "file_sha256" "2e08244774a834d0217cc86557cdf4901724efddadc65d24e8934f4ca1743e0f"} {"question" "q-325128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+1"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "877e4769e3036181573411924fec1c5ee5b9b7c36cd9dfefeccac23badc49d47" "reviewer_session" "01a118ea-3923-70c2-acd9-0830602bde5f" "rationale" "Implicit Euler with h=1 gives y_new=y+y_new^3−x_new. In the first step, y=1 and x_new=2, so moving all terms to the left gives g(y_new)=y_new−y_new^3−1+2=y_new−y_new^3+1. Original option c (index 2), the authentic worked solution, and successful grading agree. The stored formula uses the same function and subscript labels. Option a is its negative and has the same zeros, but it is a different specific function; this review preserves that distinction." "bindings" {"problem" "2239c9b9aea4d53ffb533e04966e7144d89b9608ecb18e4246490168a4f5baa0" "worked_solution" "0e163b00b24fe9254d8d4d7719af7b1cb371fbf6dd87f885bf066e3e1d35dc5a" "original_field" "c9bf0fd631e26217f931d2c03cf72967f8ce4010eab4f80da2b6f33966205506" "reviewed_field" "342939ab6e2f996d60ab8d6a0dce35e9b79935154cd1180027e7895a23e53a5c" "raw_widget" "dfdd3143dc96c5c18e1946aa8297dd72d14f1e33636838706a3a75b204746a9e"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-325128-before.json" "sha256" "e883a943e9e368b973cd797b52ac57644446f2d4a11a3eabc437163ad23542c5"} {"path" "q-325128-before.png" "sha256" "b3ba531e282a0018e5c68946ceab465c307ad2968ead4827716d8ccd5ec5728a"} {"path" "q-325128-after.png" "sha256" "9e073730585efcb4a7b0a61b77ccf7e9f8cac6f613d25c78005a63c1abb86394"} {"path" "history-q-325128.json" "sha256" "2b7d23021d97130456079cbd78c35cd66355ae86c50fa3a5edaa37a84861cbfc"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "fa77c4e37b61da8fa69e4265576c64f7ff7f5dd68969845d9dc329ba5977eb01"}]} {"question" "q-325128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-1"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055697/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "877e4769e3036181573411924fec1c5ee5b9b7c36cd9dfefeccac23badc49d47" "rationale" "Implicit Euler with h=1 gives y_new=y+y_new^3−x_new. In the first step, y=1 and x_new=2, so moving all terms to the left gives g(y_new)=y_new−y_new^3−1+2=y_new−y_new^3+1. Original option c (index 2), the authentic worked solution, and successful grading agree. The stored formula uses the same function and subscript labels. Option a is its negative and has the same zeros, but it is a different specific function; this review preserves that distinction."}] "usage" {"e-21317" [] "q-325137" [] "q-325128" [] "e-21319" []} "basis" 1824 "retractions" [[17592186229483 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y^{′} = y^{3} - x, y(1) = 1$\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx = 1$, the new value $y_{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_{new}) = 0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_{new})$ used in the first step?"] [17592186229483 :question/answer-fields 17592186227521] [17592186229484 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nConsider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2x^{2} - y^{3}, y(0.5) = 1$\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1)$. Round your answer to two decimal places.$y(1) \\approx$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186228783 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = y^3 - x\\,y, \\qquad y(1)=1$\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x = 1$, the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n1. $g(y_\\text{new}) = (y_\\text{new})^3 - 3y_\\text{new} - 1$\n2. $g(y_\\text{new}) = 3y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + 1$\n3. $g(y_\\text{new}) = 3y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 - 1$\n4. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new}$"] [17592186228783 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{new}^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (y_{new})^{3} - x_{new}y_{new} \\\\\n&= (y_{new})^{3} - x_{new}y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + (y_{new})^{3} - x_{new}y_{new} \\\\\ny_{new} - (y_{new})^{3} + x_{new}y_{new} - y_(⏟)_(g(y_{new})) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new} - y\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= y_{new} - (y_{new})^{3} + 2y_{new} - 1 \\\\\n&= 3y_{new} - (y_{new})^{3} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186228784 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2xy + y^3, \\qquad y(1.5)=-0.3$\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2)$. Round your answer to two decimal places."] [17592186228784 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1.5 = 0.5\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{new}^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (2x_{new}y_{new} + (y_{new})^{3}) \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= x_{new}y_{new} + 0.5(y_{new})^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + x_{new}y_{new} + 0.5(y_{new})^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5(y_\\text{new})^3 + (x_\\text{new}-1)y_\\text{new} + y = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 0.5\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.5(y_\\text{new})^3 + (x_\\text{new}-1)y_\\text{new} + y\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1.5 + 0.5 = 2\n$$\n\nThe old $y$ value is $y = -0.3$. Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= 0.5(y_{new})^{3} + (2 - 1)y_{new} - 0.3 \\\\\n&= 0.5(y_{new})^{3} + y_{new} - 0.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=-0.3$ to solve\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0\n$$\n\nWe'll use Newton's method.\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{g(y_\\text{new})}{g'(y_\\text{new})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\ng'(y_\\text{new}) = 1.5(y_\\text{new})^2 + 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{0.5((y_\\text{new})_n)^3 + (y_\\text{new})_n - 0.3}{1.5((y_\\text{new})_n)^2 + 1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with\n\n$$\ny(1.5) = -0.3\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y_{new})_{1} &= -0.3 \\\\\n&= (y_{new})_{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\approx 0.240529 \\\\ (y_{new})_{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\approx 0.288849 \\\\\n&= (y_{new})_{4} \\mid \\approx 0.288050\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2) \\approx 0.29\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186227522 17592186227523 17592186227524 17592186227525 17592186227526] "immutable_fields" [[17592186227521 :answer-field/correct] [17592186227521 :answer-field/id] [17592186227521 :answer-field/key] [17592186227521 :answer-field/type] [17592186227527 :answer-field/id] [17592186227527 :answer-field/key] [17592186227527 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
